  has a backend at all.

  You picked "changes arrive from elsewhere," but that bucket covers three sources with very different costs:

  - [1mDue-date and timer events[0m ("this is due in an hour", "this went overdue") need no server. They're derivable from
    task data you already have.
  - [1mAnother device[0m ("I checked this off on my phone") needs somewhere for the two devices to meet — a server, or a
    hosted sync service.
  - [1mExternal integrations[0m (calendar, GitHub, email) need something polling or receiving webhooks outside the browser —
    effectively a server.

  And one constraint worth knowing now, because people are usually surprised by it: [1ma web app can only notify you while
[0m  [1ma tab is open, unless you implement Web Push.[0m Real background delivery — notification arrives when the app is closed —
  requires the Push API, which requires a push service, VAPID keys, and a server storing subscriptions. So "notify me
  when I'm not looking" and "no backend" are mutually exclusive on the web. If every source you care about is
  timer-based [3mand[0m in-tab delivery is acceptable, this can be a pure client app; anything else pulls in a server.

  I'd recommend starting with timers plus an event log designed so a sync source can be added without redesign — you get
  a working notification inbox quickly, and the expensive part stays optional. But if multi-device is the actual
  motivation, say so now; retrofitting sync onto a client-only store is the one thing here that's genuinely painful
  later.
